# The language makes each of these a tail call. A native built-in returns to whoever called it and goes on in no tail call of its own, so the frame that waits for it bounds no chain of tail calls:
# the call becomes the call of the built-in, with the mark of the tail call that it replaces. A built-in that is written in JavaScript may end in a tail call itself: its direct call stays a tail call,
# and the inliner takes it in as it takes in any tail call. What passes the call on, as Function.prototype.call does, stays as it was
